2026-09-09T15:30:00Z RESULT: Built and backtested two new setups per user request (picked as "most likely to work" from a broader list). relative_strength (stock's 20-day return vs SPY crossing up through an outperformance threshold): not promising, negative or near-zero at all three thresholds tested (10pp -0.04%/trade, 15pp -0.15%/trade, 20pp -0.21%/trade, over 18797/13308/9613 trades respectively). vcp_breakout (breakout requiring genuine volatility contraction first -- recent-half avg daily range <= a ratio of the earlier half's, classic VCP/Minervini pattern): the most promising result this project has produced -- a real MONOTONIC improvement as the contraction requirement tightens: 0.8 ratio -> 2519 trades/-0.01%/trade (roughly breakeven), 0.7 (default) -> 1072 trades/+0.24%/trade, 0.6 (tightest) -> 383 trades/+0.79%/trade, 49.3% win rate. This is the first setup tested with a meaningfully positive result across multiple parameter values in the theoretically-predicted direction, not a single cherry-picked number. NOT promoted yet -- still no transaction costs modeled, and the best setting has a smaller sample (383 trades) -- but vcp_breakout is now automatically included in scripts/paper_trader.py's forward daily scan (shares iter_signals(), same mechanism as mean_reversion's automatic inclusion on 09-08) so real forward evidence starts accumulating immediately. relative_strength was NOT added to forward paper trading since it showed no edge (also technically needs SPY data explicitly threaded through, which the paper trader doesn't currently do). Code: added _spy_closes() helper, vcp_breakout and relative_strength setups to iter_signals(), and sensitivity-check loops to the `compare` command in scripts/backtest_ta.py.
2026-09-09T16:00:00Z NOTE: User asked whether TradingView could extend historical backtesting beyond Massive's 2-year cap. Answer: no -- the tradingview-ta integration only gives a live indicator-consensus rating, no historical bars at all, and getting real historical OHLCV from TradingView programmatically would need unofficial session-authenticated scraping tools (a bigger ToS-risk step than the public unauthenticated endpoints used so far). Instead tested Alpaca (already integrated) for daily-bar depth specifically -- confirmed live via binary search: real daily bars start ~2020-08-05 (2020-07-01 empty, 2020-08-05 real), over 3x Massive's 2-year window (~6.1 years vs ~2). Built scripts/backtest_ta.py's fetch_range_alpaca() to extend the SAME day-file cache format fetch_range() (Massive) already produces, so _load_series()/iter_signals()/run_backtest() all work unchanged on the combined multi-year dataset -- ticker-by-ticker fetch (Alpaca is per-symbol, opposite shape from Massive's per-day-whole-market call), buffered in memory and flushed every 500 tickers to bound memory, with a resumability manifest (_alpaca_fetched.json) given the lesson from the 2026-09-07 nohup fetch that died mid-run. Validated on a 3-ticker/1-week subset first, confirmed correct output shape, cleaned up test artifacts, then launched the full ~5,300-ticker/6-year fetch in the background via the harness's own run_in_background tracking (not a detached shell process, per the same 09-07 lesson).
